from merge_01d import merge

merge('entities/vladimir-putin.md', lambda n: f"""
## Satan II warning (June 2023)

Speaking to military graduates in the Grand Kremlin Palace in June 2023, Putin warned that Russia's new [[rs-28-sarmat|Satan II]] missiles — capable of carrying ten or more nuclear warheads — would be placed on combat duty "in the near future."[{n}] He described the 208-ton, 15,880 mph rocket as "unstoppable," and Western reporting characterized it as the world's longest-range intercontinental ballistic missile, able to strike a target 11,200 miles away and to devastate territories the size of England or France in a single strike.[{n}]

[[ashe-in-america|Ashe in America]] framed the announcement as a return to Cold War posturing, noting that [[ukraine|Ukraine]] and its backers had been escalating tensions with [[russia|Russia]] for months, and cautioned that whether or not the claims were accurate, "the warmongers just got their talking points for the next multibillion dollar aid package."[{n}] [[burning-bright|Burning Bright]] separately observed that US-supplied missiles used in Ukraine's counter-offensive were prompting increasing rhetoric from the Kremlin, which had begun describing the United States as a state sponsor of Nazism.[{n}]
""")

merge('entities/joe-biden.md', lambda n: f"""
## "Dictator" remark and China policy (June 2023)

At a June 20, 2023 campaign reception at a private residence in Kentfield, California, Biden referred to Chinese leader [[xi-jinping|Xi Jinping]] as a dictator, saying Xi had been embarrassed by the February 2023 shootdown of a Chinese surveillance balloon because "he didn't know it was there."[{n}] Biden said the balloon had been "blown off course up through Alaska and then down through the United States" and carried "two boxcars full of spy equipment."[{n}] The remarks came one day after Secretary of State [[antony-blinken|Antony Blinken]] concluded a visit to Beijing at which he met Xi.[{n}] China's Foreign Ministry, through spokesperson [[mao-ning|Mao Ning]], called the comments "extremely absurd and irresponsible" and "a blatant political provocation."[{n}]

[[ashe-in-america|Ashe in America]] read the episode less as a deliberate insult than as "a dementia moment," describing the wider speech as "a rambling, muddled mess" in which Biden romanticized having spent 82 recorded hours with Xi over twelve years, repeated the Charlottesville "very fine people" claim, and cited Madeleine Albright's description of America as "the essential nation."[{n}] She argued that apparent tension between Biden and Xi was "a fabricated cover for the fact that Biden is a CCP puppet."[{n}] [[burning-bright|Burning Bright]] catalogued what he called the administration's escalatory posture — refusal to recognize [[taiwan|Taiwan]] as independent, Chinese military infrastructure activity in [[cuba|Cuba]], and outreach to [[iran|Iran]] despite its [[state-department|State Department]] designation as a state sponsor of terrorism — describing these as an inverted mirror of Trump-era policy.[{n}]
""")

merge('entities/xi-jinping.md', lambda n: f"""
## Biden "dictator" remark (June 2023)

In June 2023, President [[joe-biden|Joe Biden]] publicly called Xi a dictator while recounting the February 2023 Chinese surveillance balloon shootdown, saying Xi had been embarrassed because he had not known the balloon was over the United States.[{n}] Beijing's Foreign Ministry responded that the remarks were "extremely absurd and irresponsible" and constituted "a blatant political provocation."[{n}] The exchange followed Secretary of State [[antony-blinken|Antony Blinken]]'s Beijing visit, during which Xi reportedly warned that Washington must choose between "cooperation and conflict."[{n}]
""")

merge('entities/mao-ning.md', lambda n: f"""
## Response to Biden's "dictator" remark (2023)

At a daily press briefing in Beijing on June 21, 2023, Mao Ning responded to President [[joe-biden|Joe Biden]]'s description of [[xi-jinping|Xi Jinping]] as a dictator, saying the comments were "extremely absurd and irresponsible."[{n}] She added: "It is a blatant political provocation. China expresses strong dissatisfaction and opposition."[{n}]
""")

merge('entities/antony-blinken.md', lambda n: f"""
## Beijing visit (June 2023)

Blinken concluded a visit to Beijing on June 20, 2023, meeting [[xi-jinping|Xi Jinping]] amid mounting tensions between the United States and [[china|China]].[{n}] The visit was immediately overshadowed by President [[joe-biden|Joe Biden]]'s remarks the following evening calling Xi a dictator, which drew a formal rebuke from the Chinese Foreign Ministry.[{n}] [[burning-bright|Burning Bright]] characterized the trip as having been "followed by sharp rebukes by Xi," who warned that the administration must choose between "cooperation and conflict."[{n}]
""")

merge('entities/robert-mueller.md', lambda n: f"""
## Comparison with the Durham investigation

During [[john-durham|John Durham]]'s June 21, 2023 testimony before the [[house-judiciary-committee|House Judiciary Committee]], Democratic members repeatedly contrasted Durham's inquiry with Mueller's, asking why Durham's investigation had cost more than $6 million while producing far fewer indictments than the Mueller probe.[{n}] [[adam-schiff|Adam Schiff]] continued to defend the legitimacy of the Mueller report during the hearing, a position [[justin-deschamps|Justin Deschamps]] argued was untenable given that the [[durham-report|Durham Report]] had undermined the credibility of the [[steele-dossier|Steele dossier]].[{n}]
""")

merge('concepts/russiagate-hoax.md', lambda n: f"""
## Durham's 2023 testimony

Testifying before the [[house-judiciary-committee|House Judiciary Committee]] on June 21, 2023, Special Counsel [[john-durham|John Durham]] said the [[cia|CIA]] knew that [[hillary-clinton|Hillary Clinton]] had approved a plan to tie then-candidate [[donald-trump|Donald Trump]] to Russia in order to divert attention from her classified email scandal.[{n}] He testified that the [[fbi|FBI]] had failed to "sufficiently scrutinize information it received" and did not "apply the same standards to allegations it received about the Clinton and Trump campaigns," adding that the bureau "was too willing to accept and use politically funded and uncorroborated opposition research, such as the [[steele-dossier|Steele dossier]]."[{n}]

Badlands commentators divided over the significance of the hearing. [[ryan-delarme|Ryan DeLarme]] outlined three competing readings — that Durham was compromised "fruit from the [[bill-barr|Bill Barr]] tree," that he had tried and found the system too corrupt, or that the entire sequence was theater intended to build public consent for military intervention — while holding open the possibility that the report would prove instrumental later.[{n}] [[justin-deschamps|Justin Deschamps]] argued that the hearing functioned as a deliberate lightning rod, letting the [[deep-state|Deep State]] and its media allies expend their delegitimization campaign on a comparatively thin first impression while more damning material remained undisclosed.[{n}]
""")

merge('entities/cnn.md', lambda n: f"""
## John Griffin conviction

In June 2023, former CNN television producer [[john-griffin|John Griffin]] was sentenced to more than 19 years in prison, plus 15 years of supervised release, for luring a nine-year-old girl into illegal sexual acts at his Ludlow, Vermont ski house.[{n}] Badlands coverage treated the case as emblematic of what [[justin-deschamps|Justin Deschamps]] described as an engineered "epidemic of abnormal sexual habits" cultivated by elite networks rather than an isolated crime.[{n}]
""")
